{{~##
 What every generated C++ header starts with: the banner, the include guard, the includes
 it needs, and the namespace.

 Header only, as it always was, so there is no source file and every include is a real
 dependency rather than something a .cpp happened to pull in first.

 The includes come from TypeDependencies plus whatever the file's own text reaches for.
 `<vector>` for a table's rows, `<unordered_map>` for its index, `<string>` where a field
 or a constant is one - exact, because an include a file does not need is a compile the
 consumer pays for on every translation unit that reaches it.

 A record pointing at another table's record is a pointer, and a pointer needs only an
 incomplete type, so those come from the forward header rather than an include of the other
 table. Two tables pointing at each other is a cycle otherwise, and a cycle between
 include-guarded headers does not fail loudly - it resolves differently depending on which
 translation unit got there first.
##~}}
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Generated by Tabbit. DO NOT EDIT.
//
// Changes to this file may cause incorrect behavior and will be lost if the code is
// regenerated.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#ifndef {{ include_guard }}
#define {{ include_guard }}
{{~ if includes | array.size > 0 ~}}

{{~ for line in includes ~}}
{{ line }}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if namespace_open | array.size > 0 ~}}

{{~ for line in namespace_open ~}}
{{ line }}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ end ~}}
